      en: "Pančić Peak (Pančićev vrh) is the highest summit of the Kopaonik mountain range in central Serbia, reaching an elevation of 2,017 meters. It is named after the renowned Serbian botanist Josif Pančić, who extensively studied the mountain's flora and was buried in a mausoleum at the summit according to his wishes. The mausoleum, built in 1951, suffered damage during the 1999 NATO bombings but has since been restored. Geologically, the peak consists of igneous rocks and features a broad, wind-swept plateau. Due to its commanding strategic position, the summit also hosts military radar facilities and telecommunications towers, which sometimes limits direct access to the very highest point. Nevertheless, Pančić Peak remains the focal point of the Kopaonik ski resort, with several key ski lifts terminating just below the summit. On clear days, the view from the top provides a stunning panorama that extends as far as the Prokletije mountains on the border with Montenegro.",
